 [17592186551285 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/64/64b557365c3642ae7a4206794080804bf2bbccb07230dfa46a4f415570f85329.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-5x=-5(-2)=10\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(2)^{2}=10.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd37979eada411b68755dfa85ec3b1e22585df2bff5c4bdd4b27cf06463706d6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3e7079a-616c-59e7-8130-14cd0fb2782d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551347 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66b91f732d36469ded18df5404b3f12dddad7474168efd47ddf038835b67b831.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-2x+1)=-2(-3)+1=7\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(3)^{2}+1=10.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/88/888053858d845bd47a56b953a5eb9a6f7f6b4ddffc0ecc70fa280e1a7d543f24.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=3{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)={x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}∼{\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}∼{\\pi }^{2}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed658204-766e-56cd-a12e-1cf2b14b40f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551348 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ \nTherefore, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}({\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nt))\\text{d}t \\\\ [{t}^{3}]_{0}^{x} & ∼[{\\pi }^{2}t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nt)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ {x}^{3} & ∼{\\pi }^{2}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
